@page "/docs/architecture/data-access-server"

@* Data access on the server: the store over EF Core that Static and Interactive Server pages ask, and a context
   per call for the circuit. The interface and the page are in DataAccess.razor. *@

<ArticlePage Section="architecture" Slug="data-access-server">
    <Lead>
        Static and Interactive Server pages run on the server, next to the database. One store over EF Core serves
        both, with no API between the page and the data.
    </Lead>
    <ChildContent>
        <Heading Level="2">The store</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            The page asks <Anchor Href="docs/architecture/data-access">an interface</Anchor>, and this class answers it
            with queries against your <Code>DbContext</Code>:
        </TextBlock>
        <figure class="site-diagram">
            <div class="site-diagram-scroll ib-scroll" tabindex="0" role="group" aria-label="Diagram">@((MarkupString)OnServer)</div>
            <figcaption>Static and Interactive Server: the page and the store both run on the server.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <Code Block Language="csharp" Title="Data/EfCustomerStore.cs">@Store</Code>
        <Code Block Language="csharp" Title="Program.cs">@Registration</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            <Anchor Href="docs/tools/crud"><Code>izzy add crud</Code></Anchor> writes a store of this shape for a model you
            name.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">A context for each call</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            A static page lives for one request, so its services do too. An Interactive Server page keeps its services
            for as long as its circuit. A <Code>DbContext</Code> injected into that page would stay open for the whole
            session, and two event handlers could use it at the same time. The store above creates a context for each
            call from an <Code>IDbContextFactory</Code>, which is safe on static and interactive pages. See Microsoft's
            <Anchor Href="https://learn.microsoft.com/aspnet/core/blazor/blazor-ef-core" External>Blazor with EF Core</Anchor>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Next steps</Heading>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/architecture/data-access-browser">Data access in the browser</Anchor>: what to add when a page moves to WebAssembly or Auto.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/common/data">Data and filters</Anchor>: load a Table or DataGrid's rows from a provider or EF Core.</ListItem>
        </List>
    </ChildContent>
</ArticlePage>

@code {
    private const string Store = """
        public sealed class EfCustomerStore(IDbContextFactory<ShopDb> factory) : ICustomerStore
        {
            public async Task<IReadOnlyList<CustomerRow>> ListAsync(CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
            {
                await using var db = await factory.CreateDbContextAsync(cancellationToken);
                return await db.Customers.AsNoTracking()
                    .OrderBy(c => c.Name)
                    .Select(c => new CustomerRow(c.Id, c.Name, c.City))
                    .ToListAsync(cancellationToken);
            }

            public async Task<CustomerRow?> GetAsync(int id, CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
            {
                await using var db = await factory.CreateDbContextAsync(cancellationToken);
                return await db.Customers.AsNoTracking()
                    .Where(c => c.Id == id)
                    .Select(c => new CustomerRow(c.Id, c.Name, c.City))
                    .FirstOrDefaultAsync(cancellationToken);
            }
        }
        """;

    private const string Registration = """
        // Program.cs (server)
        builder.Services.AddDbContextFactory<ShopDb>(options => options.UseSqlite(connectionString));
        builder.Services.AddScoped<ICustomerStore, EfCustomerStore>();
        """;

